finding: 1. Publication verification does not tie applied state to the deployed socket (src/cli/publish-verify.ts observePublishedServer). Its window-wide server-state counter and its boolean for any frame from the expected URL are independent. Cheap independent counterexample: copied publish-fly-verify.node.ts into a temporary test, navigated the positive page to ?server= (asserted "In-tab server · thin client"), opened an unused WebSocket to the real local endpoint with page.evaluate, and called verify(). It passed in 4.3 seconds. Thus an in-tab server plus an unrelated endpoint socket satisfies the claimed deployed-state proof. The existing wrong-endpoint test does not cover this. Require an observable association between applied state and that connection, and retain this negative control; a frame alone is insufficient. 2. The advertised one-Machine/shared-IPv4 spend bound is not enforced on supported saved or handwritten links to existing apps (src/cli/publish-fly.ts:143-179). For authorized existing apps, publish checks only the app name, skips IP allocation, and deploys with --ha=false; it never inspects Machine count or existing paid dedicated IPv4s. Fly's official https://fly.io/docs/launch/scale-count/ explicitly says the resulting scale is preserved by fly deploy except when scaled to zero; https://fly.io/docs/flyctl/deploy/ defines --ha as creating spare Machines, not scaling existing apps down. An authorized two-Machine app remains two; an existing dedicated IP also remains allocated. Refuse out-of-envelope existing inventory before mutation (do not silently destroy/scale/delete creator resources), and add a control for it. The hosted oracle starts from an absent scratch app and therefore cannot expose this gap. Independently examined the full unit/note, stage diff, runtime/refusal/config/verification tests, Fly deploy/configuration/scale documentation, and actual gh logs for 44205795423 and 44199291314. Ran 27 focused local assertions (all passed) plus the counterexample above; removed the temporary test. No hosted/personal/pre-land trial run. Notes: the real last run proves first-link collision refusal and no leftover scratch app; config, missing-token and bad-hash refusals precede remote flyctl mutation and are not dependent on fake flyctl behavior. Generated fields including concurrency and never restart are pinned. Runtime additions use Node APIs and spawned Bun, not Bun globals. SwiftShader selection affects every Linux CI publish, including client-only Cloudflare and itch, not just Fly; normal Linux without CI is unaffected. Asset/header/startup and itch document selection are otherwise unchanged. Help/skill require person confirmation before deletion. Cold-start logs show 10335.23 ms versus 2467.84 ms, with hot RTT also changing from about 5.9 ms to 95-105 ms; autostop remained about 269 versus 266 seconds. This supports changed host/network conditions but does not establish the cold-start cause: passing logs omit proxy/startup phase timings, so neither regression nor host variance is proved.
